# Fast decimal-string -> nat.
#
# Horner's rule turns each digit into acc := 10*acc + digit, and what a literal
# costs is what that step costs. Reaching for [Nat.mul_const 10] and [Nat.add]
# pays two full traversals of the accumulator per digit — one to zip the
# operands, one to ripple the carry — plus the doubled copies the multiply
# builds along the way.
#
# One traversal is enough. Walk the accumulator's bits from the low end carrying
# the value still owed at the current position: the amount pending at a bit is
# [carry + 10*bit], of which the low bit is the answer there and the rest carries
# into the next position. That carry never exceeds 10 — the fixed point of
# c -> (c + 10) / 2, entered at a digit, which is at most 9 — so it fits in four
# bits, and the whole step (carry and bit in, output bit and next carry out) is a
# lookup in a table small enough to state exhaustively. What is left per bit is
# that lookup and a cons, with no arithmetic at all.
#
# Emitting the leftover carry as the high end is what keeps the result canonical:
# the top bit of a canonical accumulator is set, so the carry leaving it is at
# least 5, and an accumulator of zero emits the digit alone.
#
# A [Memo] table over accumulators below 64 used to sit in front of this, turning
# every step of a 1- or 2-digit literal into a single lookup. It is gone: 64
# accumulators times 10 digits is ~167k nodes to build every time the module is
# loaded, which costs more than the walk it saved even on sources that are all
# small literals — and it made this function sixteen times larger than the
# tokenizer that calls it.
#
# Non-digit characters below 64 contribute their [Nat.sub_x_48] value, chars
# >= 64 contribute 0 — callers only pass digit characters.
#
# This is the [__fastest] variant (the smaller default [from_string] lives in
# nat.lamb). It lives in its own file rather than nat.lamb because it depends on
# [Memo], and [Memo] in turn depends on core [Nat] (e.g. [Nat.pred] in
# [gen_list]). Keeping it separate makes the file-level dependency graph acyclic
# (nat -> memo -> from_string) instead of forming a Nat <-> Memo cycle.
_decimal_digit = Memo.precompute_alt Memo.gen_ascii_low 0 Nat.sub_x_48
_gen_carry = Memo.gen_list Memo.gen_bool 4
_ten_plus_step = Memo.precompute_multi [_gen_carry; Memo.gen_bool] (\carry \bit
    let (Nat.add carry (Bool.match 0 10 bit)) $ \sum
    △ (List.match false (\hd \_ hd) sum) (Nat.div2 sum))
# 10 * n + carry, one pass over n's bits, low end first.
_ten_plus = self_apply $ \self \n \carry List.match
    (\_self \carry carry)
    (\hd \tl \self \carry Pair.let (_ten_plus_step carry hd) $ \bit \carry
        △ bit (self self tl carry))
    n self carry
# The inner lambda is applied immediately: it lands [acc] in a single argument
# slot instead of distributing it through the compiled chain, which measurably
# cheapens every fold iteration.
_step = \acc \c (\a _ten_plus a (_decimal_digit c)) acc
from_string__fastest = List.foldl _step 0

_to_string = Nat.to_source
from_string__fastest "0"
# = 0
from_string__fastest "42"
# = 42
from_string__fastest "255"
# = 255
from_string__fastest "65535"
# = 65535
